499 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (All children have an inherent potential to learn and grow, but a myriad of circumstances, both inside and outside of schools, prevent them from realizing their true potential. The education crisis in India is a complex issue with profound consequences, particularly affecting children in rural and remote areas, especially those from low-income and marginalized communities. Insufficient resources, untrained teachers, outdated teaching methods, and inadequate infrastructure exacerbate this problem, leading to decreased school attendance, limited access to quality education, and diminished employment prospects. Consequently, this perpetuates the cycle of poverty and denies the next generation of children their fundamental right to education.)
/E (All children have an inherent potential to learn and grow, but a myriad of circumstances, both inside and outside of schools, prevent them from realizing their true potential. The education crisis in India is a complex issue with profound consequences, particularly affecting children in rural and remote areas, especially those from low-income and marginalized communities. Insufficient resources, untrained teachers, outdated teaching methods, and inadequate infrastructure exacerbate this problem, leading to decreased school attendance, limited access to quality education, and diminished employment prospects. Consequently, this perpetuates the cycle of poverty and denies the next generation of children their fundamental right to education.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
500 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Many schools in rural and remote regions lack basic amenities such as toilets, clean drinking water, and electricity. This makes regular school attendance challenging for children, impeding their ability to learn effectively. The shortage of trained and qualified teachers, particularly in subjects like science, mathematics, and English, contributes to poor-quality education and disinterest among students. Moreover, outdated teaching methods and curricula prevalent in numerous schools hinder the development of critical thinking and creativity.)
/E (Many schools in rural and remote regions lack basic amenities such as toilets, clean drinking water, and electricity. This makes regular school attendance challenging for children, impeding their ability to learn effectively. The shortage of trained and qualified teachers, particularly in subjects like science, mathematics, and English, contributes to poor-quality education and disinterest among students. Moreover, outdated teaching methods and curricula prevalent in numerous schools hinder the development of critical thinking and creativity.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
