556 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (RBC centers cater to underprivileged children between the ages of 9 and 14 who have never attended school, are long dropouts, orphans, and semi-orphans. These centers provide 24-hour residential care, creating a safe and nurturing environment for learning. Children engage in various activities, receive education on nutrition and hygiene, and develop relationships with teachers and peers. After completing the bridge course, they are mainstreamed into nearby residential or regular government schools. 42 RBCs were operational during FY 22-23, sheltering 4,550 children, opportunity for quality learning, and a dignified future. 909 children shifted to government schools and ashramshalas from the RBCs last year \(See Table 03\).)
/E (RBC centers cater to underprivileged children between the ages of 9 and 14 who have never attended school, are long dropouts, orphans, and semi-orphans. These centers provide 24-hour residential care, creating a safe and nurturing environment for learning. Children engage in various activities, receive education on nutrition and hygiene, and develop relationships with teachers and peers. After completing the bridge course, they are mainstreamed into nearby residential or regular government schools. 42 RBCs were operational during FY 22-23, sheltering 4,550 children, opportunity for quality learning, and a dignified future. 909 children shifted to government schools and ashramshalas from the RBCs last year \(See Table 03\).)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
